# Data files are byte-exact. datasets/manifest.json records a SHA-256 for each,
# and `python datasets/build.py --verify` checks them, so any line-ending
# conversion is a correctness failure rather than a cosmetic one.
#
# Marking these as binary stops Git and Windows/OneDrive tooling from rewriting
# CRLF/LF on checkout or commit. Deliberately narrow: no global `text=auto`,
# because that would renormalise every already-committed file.
*.csv   -text
*.json  -text

# Corpus documents are byte-exact for the same reason. corpus/manifest.json and every
# corpus/kb_sets/*/manifest.json record a SHA-256 per document, and corpus/verify_sets.py
# checks them, so a CRLF/LF rewrite on checkout is a correctness failure, not cosmetic.
corpus/docs/*.md            -text
corpus/kb_sets/**/*.md      -text

# The retrieval corpus is checksummed the same way, in corpus/manifest.json, but it
# is Markdown and was not covered by the two rules above — so with core.autocrlf=true
# Git rewrote all six documents to CRLF on a Windows checkout and every working-tree
# digest disagreed with the manifest, which computes over LF bytes. Nothing was
# corrupted and Excel was never involved; the LF-normalised digest matches exactly.
#
# `text eol=lf` rather than `-text`: these are prose documents that get reviewed, so
# keeping them diffable matters, and forcing LF in the working tree is what makes the
# checksum true on every platform.
#
# After changing this, run `git add --renormalize .` once — an attribute alone does
# not rewrite files already in the working tree.
corpus/** text eol=lf
